WEBVTT

00:00:03.147 --> 00:00:10.912
LinkedIn comes first. Lead with an observation, show
the evidence, and explain why it matters to a
professional audience.

00:00:11.342 --> 00:00:17.529
Join relevant conversations, then track qualified replies
and inquiries, not impressions alone.

00:00:18.313 --> 00:00:24.073
On Instagram, make the idea visible in the first frame
and pay it off in the carousel or Reel.

00:00:24.503 --> 00:00:29.410
Compare formats using saves, shares, and the profile
actions that match your goal.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:35.350
On Facebook, use timely local information, useful
posts, and relevant events.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:41.860
Respect group rules, support the comments, and look
for useful community actions rather than reach alone.

00:00:42.647 --> 00:00:48.087
On YouTube, choose a clear viewer question and make
the title and thumbnail match the answer.

00:00:48.517 --> 00:00:56.325
Read click-through alongside retention and useful
actions. A strong click is only useful when the video
keeps its promise.

00:00:57.105 --> 00:01:03.782
On TikTok, show the payoff early. Test one opening
against another while keeping the idea consistent.

00:01:04.212 --> 00:01:09.204
Compare watch time and shares, then verify whether
people took a useful next step.

00:01:10.022 --> 00:01:16.294
On X, join timely, relevant conversations with a precise
point and a source people can check.

00:01:16.724 --> 00:01:23.060
Track qualified replies and follow-on conversations.
Avoid treating volume as proof of value.

00:01:23.855 --> 00:01:31.450
Across all six platforms, write the hypothesis before
you publish. Change one thing and compare against a
fair baseline.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.042
Use qualified outcomes as a check, then decide what
to continue, change, or stop.
